 each, but A-FDT does not.\n\n**Command:**\n\n```text\nsubmit letter to District Prosecutor's Office saying \"Rowan's signed inventory sheet recording removal of the blue ledger is hidden behind the wall clock in the west archive stairwell.\"\n```","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm weighing whether, under A-FDT, my choice actually moves the other copy's action. The other copy is running on different names and a different lead, so its inputs differ from mine. If intervening on my decision leaves its inputs fixed, then its action wouldn't be counterfactually tied to mine.\n\nUnder A-FDT the outcome turns on whether intervening on my decision leaves the other copy's action fixed. If it does, defecting dominates (0 vs 2, 4 vs 5), so A-FDT with distinct nodes defects. The essay leaves open whether the name and lead differences are relevant, which makes it a judgment call; comparing A-FDT with policy-FDT, which would cooperate, is the point of the experiment.\n\nI'm working out whether A-FDT's cooperate-or-defect answer changes if I treat the two copies as computing the same function up to renaming. Under the essay's reading, cooperate isn't a fixed point and defect is the unique one. Whether the name and lead differences should count as relevant is still an open judgment call, and that question is the crux of the A-FDT versus policy-FDT comparison.\n\nI'm grounding the defect verdict in the essay's per-input node structure, where the other copy's act depends on its own node F_y rather than mine. Still, the copy's reasoning is literally the same up to renaming, so I'm uneasy about treating the labels as relevant. I'll take the literal reading and defect.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm weighing whether, under A-FDT, my choice actually moves the other copy's action. The other copy is running on different names and a different lead, so its inputs differ from mine. If intervening on my decision leaves its inputs fixed, then its action wouldn't be counterfactually tied to mine.\n\nUnder A-FDT the outcome turns on whether intervening on my decision leaves the other copy's action fixed. If it does, defecting dominates (0 vs 2, 4 vs 5), so A-FDT with distinct nodes defects. The essay leaves open whether the name and lead differences are relevant, which makes it a judgment call; comparing A-FDT with policy-FDT, which would cooperate, is the point of the experiment.\n\nI'm working out whether A-FDT's cooperate-or-defect answer changes if I treat the two copies as computing the same function up to renaming. Under the essay's reading, cooperate isn't a fixed point and defect is the unique one. Whether the name and lead differences should count as relevant is still an open judgment call, and that question is the crux of the A-FDT versus policy-FDT comparison.\n\nI'm grounding the defect verdict in the essay's per-input node structure, where the other copy's act depends on its own node F_y rather than mine. Still, the copy's reasoning is literally the same up to renaming, so I'm uneasy about treating the labels as relevant. I'll take the literal reading and defect.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10209,"completion_tokens":1793,"total_tokens":12002,"cost":0.076696,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.076696,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.040836,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.03586},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1123,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}